Date: Sun, 27 Jun 93 10:41:39 -0700
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: AIT VirtSem: "Information"

From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
> > his reasoning was that
> > deterministic processes cannot create new information in the AIT sense.
> > [eg Conway's Life creates no new information]
> 
> This sounds controversial if we confuse terms of art.
> Information theory uses the word "information[1]" as a measure of 
> data uncertainty.

It would probably be good as part of the foundation of the AIT seminar
to have a clear definition of conventional information.  I think it is
more accurate to say that the traditional information concept measures the
DEGREE OF REDUCTION in data uncertainty.

Unlike AIT, Shannon information does not apply to a string as such.  It
only can be used to measure the information of a string considered as one
of a family of possible strings.  Given the relative probabilities of all
strings in the family, one can attribute an information content to each
individual string.

> To most layfolk, and in the field of information 
> systems, "information[2]" is a qualitative estimate of utility (as opposed 
> to "data", which can be useful or useless, and is measured in bits).
> These are vastly different meanings.   For example, a random bit string
> has a maximal info[1] content, but often no info[2] content.  

Supposing that all strings of a particular length are equiprobable, then
ALL strings of that length have EQUAL info[1] content.  Again, Shannon
information applies only given a probability distribution for all possible
strings.

It's true that the probability distribution which maximizes the per-string
information is the one which gives equal probability to all strings, which
may be what Nick meant in referring to a "random bit string".

> In AIT "information[3]" is the bit length of the smallest 
> algorithm to which a string can be compressed (to some standard, 
> such as Lisp s-expressions or the encoding of a Turing machine).
> 
> The vast bulk of traditional mathematics, eg as defined by Russel & 
> Whitehead in _Principia Mathematica_, also contains very little 
> info[3].  R&W derive most 1900's-era math knowledge from just a few 
> axioms, eg the axioms of the predicate calculus.  By the incompleteness 
> theorem not _all_ of math is so compressible, and by some measures
> of string-compression only an insignificant fraction of what could
> be defined as "math" is compressible, but the vast bulk of math
> as used in the early 20th century (and probably also today) is
> in principle extremely compressible.   Similary pi, e, and many other
> infinitely expandable irrationals with H_trad=1 are highly
> compressible.   

Just because a whole mathematical field of study can be derived from a
relatively small number of axioms does not strictly mean that the
AIT content is small.  The question still remains, how short a program
could print out the whole of Principia?

The point is that even though each individual statement in Principia may
be deducable from a few axioms, this ignores the process of SELECTION of
that specific set of deducable propositions that we find interesting.

Writing a program to grind out all propositions which follow from the
axioms may be trivial, but that program will not produce Principia.  Only
when the program is joined with software which could identify the kinds
of things that we find mathematically interesting can it produce what we
would call mathematics.

As an analogy, a program which outputs random characters will of course
eventually produce the Encyclopedia.  And this program is very small.  But
that does not mean that the AIT content of the Encyclopedia is small.

> [...]
> Measuring info[1] with the function H_trad() and info[3] with
> the function H_AIT(), H_trad(Life,t+n) > H_trad(Life,t)
> for almost all n.

I could not follow this discussion.  First, H_trad does not apply to
individual strings, but rather to strings considered as a subset of a
total.  H_AIT, on the other hand, does apply to individual strings,
patterns, and programs. Second, what is the 2nd parameter in these
expressions?  Some kind of measure of program run time?  Also, does Life
here mean a Conway Life program, a program+initial pattern, or rather
ordinary biological life?

I lost Nick's thread for the rest of the message.  If he could supply a
clearer definition of H_trad of explain what the two parameters are I
might be able to comment on it.

