Date: Sun, 27 Jun 93 20:47:24 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: QM/TOE: Many-Worlds & Dreams of a Final Theory

Eric S. Raymond says:

> Interesting article --- but I must protest that this is
> anti-predictive only if one's notion of "prediction" is fairly narrow
> (possibly Popper's was).  Retrodiction is simply a form of prediction
> checked against data that happens already to have been gathered.

Not quite.  The important distinction (and this is important, which is
why I'm re-emphasizing it) between retrodiction and prediction lies in
the mind of the experimenter, their theories and hence their own bias
in eliminating experimental bias.  As long as the results gathered are
in disagreement with theory the experimenter is motivated to repeat,
refine or even redesign the apparatus and setup.  When theory and
experiment agree the experimenter relaxes and publishes without further
ado.  I'm not criticising this, we're all human and I'd do the same. 
But the result of human nature being what it is means that we should
place more reliability on retrodictive successes than predictive
successes.  Data gathered in the absence of a theory is more likely to
be error free.

What galvanised Halley (and the rest of the Royal Society) into pushing
Newton to publish was the retrodictive explanation of elliptical,
Keplerian planetary orbits and tides.  The prediction of the return of
Halley's comet (made in 1704, verified in 1758) was incidental to the
acceptance of Newtonian gravity/mechanics, yet this was an extension of
the theory of gravity from the planetary to cometary realm (a much
stronger claim to science by Popperian standards).

With regards to Einstein's prediction of the eclipse data (in 1919)
general relativity was pretty much accepted by the successful
explanation of the 'anomalous' perihelion advance of Mercury (and also
by GR's axiomatic simplicity).  Eddington and others would not have
organised two expensive trips to distant parts of the world had they not
been convinced about what they would find.  And the data they did gather
was not as unambiguous as folklore asserts.  Weinberg reports that some
plates were in flat contradiction with Einstein - these were attributed
to a mirror defect (I think, I don't have a copy of _Dreams of a Final
Theory_ at hand) and never found their way into the published data.

That's not to say that the eclipse experiments didn't have a dramatic
impact on public consciousness - they did.  Newspapers were inundated
with reports of people shining torches across fields at night and
claiming to see the bending of their beams etc.  I think the reason for
the public impact was post-war weariness and a "let's not be beastly to
the Germans" attitude.  Here was a theory by an absent-minded German
Professor, championed by an English astronomer (director of the
Cambridge observatory).  The public loved it.  That the data didn't
quite fit was irrelevant.

