Date: Sat, 26 Jun 93 11:47:45 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: The Game of "Go" as an Example

In this extended reply, I will use "Go" as a touchstone for discussing
string complexity, fitness functions, and the answer to the question of how
complexity can emerge from simplicity.

(I picked "Go" because many Extropians play it, it's a simple to describe
formal system, and yet it exhibits enormous complexity. It's easier to
think about Go than it is to think about the complexity of e.coli, or
humans, or whatever.)

Harry Shapiro wrote:

>a conscious being, Timothy C. May wrote:
>> In GA and GP systems (genetic algorithms, genetic programming), string
>> complexity does in fact increase. One can visualize some particular
>> sequence like this:
>> 
>> Initial String:                 11111111111111111......1111111
>...
>> Later (more mutations, cross.): 01100110101110110......1010001
>> 
>
>> implying that the initiall string is highly regular, such as all 1s or all
>
>Certainly in the a-life program, the resulting generations "Seem" more
>complex. "life: goes from a very simply algorithim to very dense
>complex bit of code. Does this count a 'complexity'?

Yes, exactly, this is "complexity."

Where this complexity comes from is not surprising: it comes from the
"landscape" an organism/agent/etc. is wandering around in. The survival of
a string (a genome for an organism, a set of programs for an AI, a strategy
in a game) and its "differential reproduction" (whether it survives long
enough to breed and hence replicate/pass on its string) depends on its
"fitness function."

Thus, in GA terms, we may start with a "stupid" string, one that barely
knows how to find its way to the local watering hole. (I apologize for
shifting gears to specific examples, like watering holes, differential
reproduction, etc. These arguments could be put in abstract terms, but the
mental model is important before tackling purely formal representations.)

The "stupid" string (idealized as "11111....1111", though of course it is
most likely something much more random looking) competes against other
strings, themselves probably roughly as stupid (for our model here). Those
who get to the watering hole, or the food sources, or whatever (win more
games of "Go," put pieces of machinery together better, etc.), get to breed
and reproduce (mutations and crossovers, in Holland's terms).

Eventually, the theory (and practice) goes, a "more fit" organism/agent
emerges. The string representing its genome/programming has more
"structure." (Even if the string looks "about as random" as it did when it
started, the bits are now in some important sense more "critical"--randomly
twiddling them makes a bigger difference than at the beginning, for
example.).

Does Increased Complexity Really Happen?

There seem to be two important cases:

1. An agent (=organism, critter, genome) exploring a fitness function
landscape, such as biological agents face in the real environment.

2. An agent playing a formal game, like chess or Go. The axioms are set
(the rules of the game), and one or more agents are proving theorems and
making conjectures in this situation (though of course they most likely
don't have our sense of proving theorems! To them it probably looks like
the situation in #1.)

In the first case, the string complexity packs in knowledge gained about
the evironment. Not as formal theorems, but as bit twiddlings that
(somehow) enhance survivability (actually, all that matters is differential
reproduction...survival beyond breeding and upbringing is unimportant,
however unExtropian this may sound!).

Complexity thus reflects the "thrown" (given) complexity of the real world,
with its geometrical laws, ecologies, sources of food and water, other
critters, and on and on.

In the second case, it may be argued that all of the formal implications
are contained in the initial axioms. This was the Hilbert/Russell model of
starting with basic axioms and then "grinding out" all of the formal,
logical implications. Godel demolished this hope, but we don't even need
Godel to show the hopelessness of this. (As Chaitin has noted, "Hilbert was
not a twit." Hilbert had no illusions that such mechanistic proof would
replace mathematics, nor did he think it would be easy. He just thought it
was possible, which it is not.)

Consider the game of "Go," played with simple rules governing the placement
of black and white stones on a grid. Simpler rules are scarcely imaginable,
and yet the game has been played for several thousand years and generates
an enormous literature every year. It is likely that alien civilizations,
if they exist, have games very similar to Go.

So, given that there are only a few rules, where does the "real complexity"
come from? 

(I'm now touching on Hal Finney's points about where complexity comes from,
and whether "deterministic" systems can have real complexity. Go is
certainly deterministic, in the sense of rules and no quantum-mechanical
uncertainty. But it has an amazing property--that of multiple agents, whose
internal thoughts and goals are unknown--that is, I think, of profound
importance.)

Playing the game, and adopting strategies that seem to work, is jsut like
genetic programming or genetic algorithms. Theorems are proved ("A group
with two eyes lives.") and conjectures/probabalistic statements are made
("If you control 3 corners, resign.") Expert players have tens of thousands
of patterns they have seen, thousands of theorems/strategies/heuristics,
and vast amounts of tactical (local) knowledge. All from those simple
rules.

Since it's a formal, deterministic system (modulo the point about different
agents, made above), why not just "computerize" it?

First, there are about 10^700 legally possible Go games, not counting the
ones with long cycles of repetitious behavior (capture, recapture,
continuing). This contrasts with "only" about 10^130 legally possible games
of chess. If every single particle in the Universe, all 10^70 of them, was
somehow made into a powerful computer, then each would be responsible for
10^630 games!!

(These numbers, for a mere 19 x 19 grid, the standard Go game size, are
enormously large. I urge readers to think about how these numbers--which
are utterly outside our ability to ever compute directly--relate to the "in
principle we could calculate the future evolution of the Universe" sorts of
speculations. To my mind, neither quantum mechanics nor chaos theory is
needed to prove that determinism is a dead idea.)

Bennett's idea of "logical depth" is applicable here. Two Go agents, each
starting with some string (who knows how many bits...it's the "Go programs"
that might be run on some machine) representing its strategy, begin to
play. They follow the logical rules of Go, but they also have to anticipate
what is going on in the "mind" of their opponent, which almost certainly
sees the game differently.

Just as centralized economies fail to take into account the preferences of
economic agents (how's this for a change of gears?), so too do ideas of
"automating" a game fail to take into account the differing internal models
and capabilities of agents. This is, in some important sense, a "bound on
the rationality of agents," as Herbert Simon has put it.

(To ward off flames, this has _nothing_ to do with mystical ideas that
Man's rationality is overrated, that Reason must yield to Faith, etc. So,
please, no Randian arguments. In fact, this view of bounded rationality--so
evident in the Go game--validates the "distributed computation" models of
Hayek, Friedman, et. al. This deserves an entire essay, so I won't go into
it here. I think the directions are clear.)

So, two agents playing Go develop winning strategies, compete with other
agents who are also learning, figure out tricks and pitfalls, and so on. A
Platonist might even say there really "is" a "Go landscape" out there, a
multidimensional surface filled with local hills and valleys, crevasses,
watering holes, food sources, walls, and all sorts of other "structure." Go
agents are like biological critters, wandering and reproducing in this
landscape, and learning where the "upward" paths are(upward means winning,
a la hill-climbing algorithms such as the Metropolis algorithm, Hopfield
networks, simualated annealing, and all the recent results out of Holland's
and Koza's work on GAs and GP).

(For some concreteness, imagine each Go agent starting with a 1000-bit long
string, a fairly simple string compared to the 4 megabyte genome of e.
coli. The bits are on-off switches for various possible Go strategies, or
are values (by using 8 bits, for 256 levels of some parameter, for
example)...such as how far to look ahead, how to weight the loss of stones,
how much importance to place on creating "life" (a Go term, meaning
survival of a group of stones), and so on. 

These strings might start out as initially "random," or as some fixed
pattern. But, as games are played with various settings of these bits
(mutations can change them, or breeding with other strings a la GAs), the
string changes. It's not clear that a 1000-bit string can ever play
adequate Go, but you get the point. A note for the curious: Bruce Wilcox's
"Nemesis" program does in fact have a "control panel" that allows many
parameters to be twiddled by the user, altering the weighting Nemesis uses.
In a sense, the bit strings are the inputs to a "Go engine" (a kind of
Turing machine, loosely speaking). Obviously, the "Go engine" is not set in
concrete, and should be coded as part of the bit string...things get
confusing, especially when attempting to separate inputs to a machine from
the machine itself. In truth, they're both part of the overall approach.)

Enormous complexity--or logical depth, in Bennett's terms--emerges out of
ostensibly very simple rules. ("Emergent behavior" is of course a
fascinating idea. I sprang this Go example on William Calvin at the first
A-LIFE conference in 1987 and he thought it was right on.)

Well, this has gone on too long.

The connection with Chaitin and AIT is, I think, fairly clear. By doing a
lot of "computation," complexity emerges. Bit strings (a representation of
the actual programming, which is, of course, machine-dependent,
language-dependent, and all that good stuff) become more complex, _less_
random in one sense (the bits matter more), and _more_ random in another
sense (the strings are less compressible, more algorithmically random).

In a sense, a winning Go agent--one that has played a lot of games and does
well--has *no shorter description of its strategy* than just the strategy
(bit string) itself!

That is, you can't take the winning Go agent and say, "Yeah, but tell me in
a few simple rules what your strategy is."

I'm not saying all such agents/strategies/etc. are like this, but evolved
agents are quite likely to be this way. If so, this has some important
implications for AI and fits with the failures of the rule-based paradigm
and the current ascendency of the distributed computation, evolutionary
model (such as: neural nets, genetic programming, connectionist
models...mixed in various ways).

Complexity, algorithmic information theory, artificial life, fitness
functions, genetic algorithms, logical depth, machine learning,neural nets,
emergent behavior, markets, agents....all are related in exciting ways.

I hope this extended reply helps in some way.

-Tim May
